== Tenderness of Spirit

IX. Gracious affections soften the heart, and are attended and followed with a Christian tenderness of spirit.

False affections, however much persons may seem to be melted by them while they are new, still tend in the end to harden the heart. A disposition to some kinds of passions may be established, such as those that imply self-seeking, self-exaltation and opposition to others. But false affections, with the delusion that attends them, finally tend to dull the mind and shut it against those affections in which tenderness of heart consists. And their effect at last is this: in the settled frame of their minds, persons become less affected by their present and past sins and less conscientious about future sins. They are less moved by the warnings and cautions of God’s word, or by God’s chastisements in his providence. They are more careless of the frame of their hearts, and of the manner and tendency of their behavior. They are less quick-sighted to discern what is sinful, and less afraid of the appearance of evil, than they were while they were under legal awakenings and fears of hell. Now that they have had such and such impressions and affections, they have a high opinion of themselves and regard their state as safe. So they can be much more at ease than before in living in the neglect of duties that are troublesome and inconvenient. They are much slower and more partial in obeying difficult commands. They are nowhere near so alarmed at the sight of their own defects and transgressions. They are emboldened to indulge themselves more with respect to the labor, the painful care and the exactness of their walk. They yield more easily to temptations and to the urgings of their lusts. And they take far less care of their behavior when they come into the holy presence of God, in the time of public or private worship. Formerly, perhaps, under legal convictions, they took great pains in religion and denied themselves in many things. But now that they think themselves out of danger of hell, they largely put off the burden of the cross, spare themselves the trouble of difficult duties, and allow themselves more of the comfort of enjoying their ease and their lusts.

Persons like these, instead of embracing Christ as their #emph[Savior from sin,] trust in him as the #emph[Savior of their sins.] Instead of fleeing to him as their refuge #emph[from their spiritual enemies,] they make use of him as the defense #emph[of their spiritual enemies from God,] and to strengthen them against him. They make Christ the minister of sin, the great officer and deputy of the devil, to strengthen his interest and make him, above all things in the world, strong against JEHOVAH. So they may sin against him with good courage and without any fear, being effectually secured against the restraints of his most solemn warnings and most fearful threatenings. They trust in Christ to preserve to them the quiet enjoyment of their sins, and to be their shield to defend them from God’s displeasure. Meanwhile they come close to him, even to his bosom, the place of his children, to fight against him with their deadly weapons hidden under their robes. #footnote[These are hypocrites who believe, but fail in regard to the use of the gospel and of the Lord Jesus. And we read of these in #emph[Jude] 3, namely, of some men #emph[who turned grace into licentiousness.] For in this the exceeding evil of a man’s heart appears: that not only the law, but also the glorious gospel of the Lord Jesus, works in him all kinds of unrighteousness. And it is all too common for men at the first work of conversion to cry, oh, for grace and Christ, and afterward to grow licentious, to live and lie in the breaking of the law, and to take their warrant for their course from the gospel. #emph[Shepard]’s parable Part. I. P. 126. Again, p. 232, Mr. #emph[Shepard] speaks of such hypocrites as those who are like strange eggs put into the same nest where honest men have lived, and so have been hatched there. While they are young they keep to their nest, and live by crying and opening their mouths wide after the Lord and the food of his word. But when their wings are grown, and they have gained some affections, some knowledge, some hope of mercy, they are hardened by these to fly from God. And he adds: Can that man be good whom God’s grace makes worse? Again, Part II. P. 167: When men flee to Christ in times of peace so that they may keep their sins with greater peace of conscience, so that sin makes them flee to Christ as well as misery, not that they may destroy and abolish sin, but that they may be kept in their sins with peace, then men may be said to lay hold of Christ only by a seeming faith.—Many a heart secretly says this: If I can have my sin, and peace, and a quiet conscience for the present, and God merciful to pardon it afterward. That is why he relies (as he says) only on the mercy of God in Christ. And now this hardens and blinds him, and makes him secure, and his faith is sermon-proof: nothing stirs him.—And if it were not for their faith they would despair, but this keeps them up. And now they think that if they have any trouble of mind, it is the devil troubling them. And so they make Christ and faith protectors of sin, not purifiers from sin, which is most dreadful: turning grace into licentiousness, as they did with sacrifice. So these would sin under the shadow of Christ, because the shadow is good and sweet, #emph[Mich.] 3. 11. They had subtle, sly ends in good duties, for a man’s sin may lie there; “yet they lean upon the #smallcaps[Lord].”—When the money changers came into the temple: #emph[you are making it a den of robbers.] Robbers, when they are hunted, flee to their den or cave, and there they are safe against all searchers and hue and cry. So it is here. But Christ whipped them out. So when men are pursued with the cries and fears of conscience, away to Christ they go, as to their den: not as saints, to pray and mourn out the life of their sin there, but to preserve their sin. This is vile. Will the Lord receive such?] Yet some of these, at the same time, make a great profession of love to God and assurance of his favor, and of great joy in tasting the sweetness of his love.

It was in this way that #emph[those] trusted in Christ whom the apostle #emph[Jude] speaks of, #emph[who crept in] among the saints #emph[unnoticed,] but were really #emph[ungodly men, turning the grace of God into a license for immorality] (Jude 4). These are the ones who trust in their being righteous, and because God has promised that #emph[the righteous will surely live,] that is, will certainly be saved, they are emboldened to #emph[commit iniquity.] God threatens them in Ezekiel 33:13: “If I tell the righteous man that he will surely live, but he then trusts in his righteousness and commits iniquity, then none of his righteous works will be remembered; he will die because of the iniquity he has committed.”

Gracious affections have a quite contrary tendency: they turn a heart of stone more and more into a heart of flesh. A holy love and hope are principles far more efficacious on the heart than a slavish fear of hell, to make it tender, to fill it with a dread of sin, or of whatever might displease and offend God, and to engage it to watchfulness, care and strictness. Gracious affections, as was observed before, flow out of a contrite heart, or (as the word means) a bruised heart, bruised and broken with godly sorrow. This makes the heart tender, as bruised flesh is tender and easily hurt. Godly sorrow has much greater influence to make the heart tender than mere legal sorrow from selfish principles.

The tenderness of the heart of a true Christian is beautifully pictured by our Savior when he compares such a person to a little child. The flesh of a little child is very tender, and so is the heart of one who is newborn. This is represented in what we are told of #emph[Naaman]’s cure from his leprosy by washing in the #emph[Jordan] at the prophet’s direction, which was undoubtedly a type of the renewing of the soul by washing in the basin of regeneration. We are told in 2 Kgs 5:14 that he “went down and dipped himself in the Jordan seven times, according to the word of the man of God, and his flesh was restored and became like that of a little child.” Not only is the flesh of a little child tender, but his mind is tender. A little child’s heart is easily moved, worked on and bowed; so is a Christian’s in spiritual things. A little child is quick to be moved by sympathy, to weep with those who weep, and can hardly bear to see others in distress; so it is with a Christian (John 11:35; Rom 12:15; 1 Cor 12:26). A little child is easily won by kindness; so is a Christian. A little child is easily moved to grief by earthly evils; his heart melts and he begins to weep. So tender is the heart of a Christian with regard to the evil of sin. A little child is easily frightened at the appearance of outward evils, or of anything that threatens to hurt it; so a Christian is quick to be alarmed at the appearance of moral evil, and of anything that threatens to hurt the soul. A little child, when it meets enemies or fierce beasts, is not inclined to trust its own strength, but flees to its parents for refuge; so a saint is not self-confident in engaging spiritual enemies, but flees to Christ. A little child is apt to suspect evil in places of danger: afraid in the dark, afraid when left alone or far from home. So a saint is apt to feel his spiritual dangers deeply, to be wary of himself, full of fear when he cannot see his way plain before him, afraid to be left alone and to be at a distance from God. Prov 28:14: “Blessed is the man who always fears, but he who hardens his heart falls into trouble.” A little child is apt to be afraid of those over him, to dread their anger, and to tremble at their frowns and threats; so is a true saint with respect to God. Ps 119:120: “My flesh trembles in awe of You; I stand in fear of Your judgments.” Isa 66:2: “This is the one I will esteem: he who is humble … who trembles at My word.” Verse 5: “You#super[+] who tremble at His word, hear the word of the #smallcaps[Lord].” Ezra 9:4: “Then everyone who trembled at the words of the God of Israel gathered around me.” 10:3: “According to the counsel of my lord and of those who tremble at the command of our God.” A little child approaches those over him with awe; so the saints approach God with holy awe and reverence. Job 13:11: “Would His majesty not terrify you#super[+]? Would the dread of Him not fall upon you#super[+]?” Holy fear is so much the nature of true godliness that Scripture calls it by no name more often than the #emph[fear of God.]

So gracious affections do not tend to make men bold, forward, noisy and boisterous, but rather to make them #emph[speak with trembling] (Hos 13:1: “When Ephraim spoke with trembling, he was exalted in Israel. But he incurred guilt through Baal, and he died”), and to clothe them with a kind of holy fear in all their behavior toward God and man, in keeping with Psalm 2:11; 1 Pet 3:15; 2 Cor 7:15; Eph 6:5; 1 Pet 3:2; Rom 11:20.

But here some may object and say: Is there no such thing as a holy boldness in prayer and the duties of divine worship? I answer that there is doubtless such a thing, and it is chiefly to be found in eminent saints, persons of great degrees of faith and love. But this holy boldness is not in the least opposed to #emph[reverence,] though it is opposed to #emph[disunion] and #emph[servility.] It abolishes or lessens the disposition that arises from #emph[moral distance] or #emph[alienation,] and also from #emph[distance of relation,] such as that of a slave. But it does not at all lessen the disposition that befits our #emph[natural distance,] by which we are infinitely inferior. No boldness in poor sinful worms of the dust who have a right view of God and of themselves will prompt them to approach God with less fear and reverence than the spotless and glorious angels in heaven, who cover their faces before his throne (Isa 6, at the beginning). #emph[Rebekah] (who in her marriage with #emph[Isaac] was, in almost all its circumstances, plainly a great type of the church, the bride of Christ), when she meets #emph[Isaac,] gets down from her camel, takes a veil and covers herself. And this though she was brought to him as his bride, to be with him in the nearest relation and most intimate union in which human beings are ever united to one another. #footnote[Dr. #emph[Ames,] in his cases of conscience, book III. Chap. Iv., speaks of a holy modesty in the worship of God as one sign of true humility.] #emph[Elijah,] that great prophet who had so much holy familiarity with God, at a time of special nearness to God, even when he conversed with him on the mountain, wrapped his face in his cloak. This was not because he was terrified with any slavish fear by the terrible #emph[wind] and #emph[earthquake] and #emph[fire.] It was after these were all over, and God spoke to him as a friend #emph[in a still, small voice.] 1 Kgs 19:12, 13: “And after the fire came a still, small voice. When Elijah heard it, he wrapped his face in his cloak.” And #emph[Moses,] with whom God spoke face to face as a man speaks with his friend, and who was distinguished from all the prophets by the familiarity with God he was admitted to, did the same. At the time when he was brought nearest of all, when God showed him his glory on that same mountain where he afterward spoke to #emph[Elijah,] Moses #emph[hurried to bow his head toward the earth, and worshiped] (Exod 34:8). In some persons there is a most unfitting and intolerable boldness in their addresses to the great JEHOVAH, a show of holy boldness and a display of eminent nearness and familiarity. The very thought of it would make them shrink into nothing with horror and confusion if they saw the distance there is between God and them. They are like the #emph[Pharisee,] who boldly came up near, confident of his own eminence in holiness. If they saw their vileness, they would instead be more like the #emph[tax collector,] who #emph[stood at a distance, and would not even lift up his eyes to heaven, but beat his breast, saying, God, have mercy on me, a sinner.] It befits sinful creatures such as we are to approach a holy God (with faith, indeed, and without terror, yet) with contrition, and penitent shame and confusion of face. It is foretold that this would be the disposition of the church in the time of her highest privileges on earth, in her latter day of glory, when God would remarkably comfort her by revealing his covenant mercy to her. Ezek 16:60 to the end: “I will establish an everlasting covenant with you. Then you will remember your ways and be ashamed. … So I will establish My covenant with you, and you will know that I am the #smallcaps[Lord], so that when I make atonement for all you have done, you will remember and be ashamed and never again open your mouth because of your disgrace, declares the Lord #smallcaps[God].” The woman we read of in the seventh chapter of #emph[Luke] was an eminent saint, and by Christ’s own testimony (verse 47) she had much of that true love which casts out fear. She approached Christ in a lovely and acceptable way when she came with that humble modesty, reverence and shame, when she stood at his feet weeping #emph[behind him,] as one not fit to appear before his face, and washed his feet with her tears.

One reason why gracious affections are attended with this tenderness of spirit that has been spoken of is that true grace tends to promote convictions of conscience. Persons usually have convictions of conscience before they have any grace. And if afterward they are truly converted, and have true repentance, and joy and peace in believing, this tends to put an end to #emph[terrors,] but has no tendency to put an end to #emph[convictions of sin;] rather it tends to increase them. It does not dull a man’s conscience; it makes it more sensitive. It makes it discern more easily and thoroughly the sinfulness of what is sinful, receive a greater conviction of the heinous and dreadful nature of sin, and be capable of a quicker and deeper sense of it. It makes him more convinced of his own sinfulness and the wickedness of his heart, and so it tends to make him more wary of his heart. Grace tends to give the soul a further and better #emph[conviction] of the same things about sin that it was #emph[convinced] of under a legal work of the Spirit of God: namely, its great contrariety to the will, the law and the honor of God, the greatness of God’s hatred of it and displeasure against it, and the dreadful punishment it exposes to and deserves. And not only that: it #emph[convinces] the soul of something further about sin that it saw nothing of while it was only under legal #emph[convictions,] and that is the infinitely hateful nature of sin, and its dreadfulness on that account. And this makes the heart tender with respect to sin, like #emph[David]’s heart, which struck him when he had cut off the corner of #emph[Saul]’s robe. The heart of a true penitent is like a burnt child that dreads the fire. On the contrary, the one who has had a counterfeit repentance and false comforts and joys is like iron that has been suddenly heated and quenched: it becomes much harder than before. A false conversion puts an end to convictions of conscience, and so either takes away or greatly diminishes the conscientiousness that was shown under a work of the law.

All gracious affections tend to promote this Christian tenderness of heart that has been spoken of: not only a godly sorrow, but also a gracious joy. Ps 2:11: “Serve the #smallcaps[Lord] with fear, and rejoice with trembling.” So also does a gracious hope. Ps 33:18: “Surely the eyes of the #smallcaps[Lord] are on those who fear Him, on those whose hope is in His loving devotion.” And Ps 147:11: “The #smallcaps[Lord] is pleased with those who fear Him, who hope in His loving devotion.” Indeed, the most confident and assured hope that is truly gracious has this tendency. The higher a holy hope is raised, the more there is of this Christian tenderness. The banishing of a slavish fear by a holy assurance is attended with a proportionate increase of a reverential fear. The lessening of the fear of the fruits of God’s displeasure in future punishment is attended with a proportionate increase of the fear of his displeasure itself; the lessening of the fear of hell, with an increase of the fear of sin. The vanishing of doubts about the person’s state is attended with a proportionate increase of wariness of his heart, a distrust of its strength, wisdom, stability, faithfulness and so on. The less apt he is to be afraid of natural evil, having “his heart … steadfast, trusting in the #smallcaps[Lord],” and so not fearing “bad news,” the more apt he is to be alarmed at the appearance of moral evil, the evil of sin. As he has more holy boldness, so he has less self-confidence and less of a forward, presuming boldness, and more modesty. As he is surer than others of deliverance from hell, so he has more of a sense that he deserves it. He is less apt than others to be shaken in faith, but more apt than others to be moved by solemn warnings, by God’s frowns, and by the calamities of others. He has the firmest comfort, but the softest heart; he is richer than others, but poorest of all in spirit; the tallest and strongest saint, but the least and tenderest child among them.
